        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Ready Seal is the fence stain most homeowners should buy: it sprays evenly, hides lap marks and fades without peeling. SuperDeck is the paint-store alternative with more color control, Olympic Maximum is the easy big-box value, BEHR Solid rescues old and mismatched fences, DEFY Extreme suits low-VOC states, Cabot suits cedar showpieces and Thompson's covers utility fences on a budget.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The job itself decides how well any of them works. Let new treated wood dry, clean and brighten old fences, buy for rough-wood coverage, work post to post in the shade and back-brush as you go. For the deck next to the fence, see our <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">deck stain rankings</a>.
          </p>
